 Strip personal data out of log files before sharing them.
 
 ```
-python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [FILE ...]
 ```
 
 Each `FILE` is read in the order given, or standard input when no file is
 given (a `FILE` of `-` also means standard input). Every line is written to
-standard output with three kinds of data replaced; everything else on the
-line, including lines with nothing to redact, is passed through byte for
-byte.
+standard output — or, with `--out-dir`, to a file of the same name in `DIR`
+— with four kinds of data replaced; everything else on the line, including
+lines with nothing to redact, is passed through byte for byte.
 
 | Data | Replacement | Rule |
 | --- | --- | --- |
 | E-mail address | `[EMAIL]` | `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` → `[EMAIL]` |
 | IPv4 address | `[IP]` | four dot-separated octets, each 0-255 |
 | Payment card number | `[CARD]` | 13-19 digits, optionally in groups separated by single spaces or hyphens, passing the Luhn checksum |
+| Phone number | `[PHONE]` | international format: a `+` followed by 8-15 digits, as in `+14155552671` |
 
 ```console
 $ printf 'user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n' | python -m logredact
@@ -25,13 +26,43 @@ user [EMAIL] from [IP]
 $ printf 'paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123\n' | python -m logredact
 paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123
 
-$ printf 'a@b.io 1.2.3.4\n' | python -m logredact --count
-[EMAIL] [IP]
-emails=1 ips=1 cards=0          # on standard error
+$ printf 'call +14155552671 now\n' | python -m logredact
+call [PHONE] now
+
+$ printf 'a@b.io +442071838750\n' | python -m logredact --count
+[EMAIL] [PHONE]
+emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1     # on standard error
 ```
 
-`--count` prints `emails=N ips=N cards=N` to standard error once all output
-has been written.
+`--count` prints `emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N` to standard error once
+all output has been written. The counts are totals over every `FILE`.
+
+## Writing to a folder
+
+`--out-dir DIR` sends the redacted lines of each `FILE` to a file of the
+same name in `DIR` instead of to standard output, and prints `wrote N files`
+to standard output once every `FILE` has been processed.
+
+```console
+$ python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log web.log
+wrote 2 files
+
+$ cat clean/app.log
+user [EMAIL]
+```
+
+* Only the name of the `FILE` is used, so `logs/2026/app.log` becomes
+  `DIR/app.log`.
+* `DIR` is created if it does not exist, along with any missing parent
+  directories.
+* An existing file of that name in `DIR` is replaced.
+* `N` counts output files, so two `FILE`s with the same name (such as
+  `one/app.log two/app.log`) share one output file holding the second one's
+  lines, and `N` is 1. Both are still included in the `--count` totals.
+* `--count` is unaffected: `wrote N files` goes to standard output and the
+  summary to standard error.
+* Standard input has no name to write to, so `--out-dir` needs at least one
+  named `FILE` and rejects a `FILE` of `-`.
 
 Requires Python 3.11 or newer and nothing outside the standard library.
