+earlier placeholders contain no digits, so a later pass cannot match inside
+them. Where two rules could claim the same digits the earlier one wins, so
+a `+`-tagged address such as `jane+14155552671@example.com` stays a single
+`[EMAIL]`, and a `+` followed by 13 to 15 digits that happens to pass the
+Luhn check is a `[PHONE]`. A 16-to-19-digit card is past the phone range, so
+`+4111111111111111` is still `+[CARD]`.
+
 Lines are processed one at a time, so arbitrarily large files stream without
-being held in memory. Input is decoded as UTF-8 with `surrogateescape`, and
-line endings are preserved as-is, so undecodable bytes and CRLF files
-survive the round trip.
+being held in memory, whether the destination is standard output or a file
+in `--out-dir`. Input is decoded as UTF-8 with `surrogateescape`, and line
+endings are preserved as-is, so undecodable bytes and CRLF files survive the
+round trip.
 
